Bugfix: The assembly of a large web upload no longer depends on the open connection

After the last chunk the web interface sends one MOVE request that makes the
server assemble the file. That request stayed open until the assembly was
done - minutes for a few gigabytes, hours with a slow virus scanner - and
every proxy timeout, PHP-FPM reload or page reload in between killed the
assembly and left a stale .part file behind.

The asynchronous MOVE (OC-LazyOps) is now always available for requests that
ask for it; `dav.enable.async` still only controls the capability announced
to sync clients. The server answers with 202 at once (Content-Length 0, and
`fastcgi_finish_request()` under PHP-FPM so nginx releases the connection),
assembles the file after the response and records the job status, which the
web interface polls until it is finished. The poll accepts the initial status
`init`, and a PHP error during the assembly is recorded as job error instead
of leaving the status at `started`. The assembly stream no longer triggers
the PHP 8.4 deprecation for `fopen()` with a null third argument.
